<!-- ============ 3. WEEKEND PLAN ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="kicker">Chapter 1</div>
  <h2>The weekend plan</h2>
  <p class="lede">About four hours in total, split into four sessions. Reading first, decisions second, clicking buttons last.</p>
  <div class="timer">
    <div><div class="t">Sat AM</div><div class="h">Understand (60 min)</div>
      <ul><li>Read chapters 2 to 6 <span class="pg">p.4&ndash;8</span></li><li>Check your workplace plan: is there one, and a match?</li><li>Decide which account comes first</li></ul></div>
    <div><div class="t">Sat PM</div><div class="h">Compare (60 min)</div>
      <ul><li>Read chapter 7 <span class="pg">p.9</span></li><li>Fill in the worksheet for 2 or 3 providers <span class="pg">p.10</span></li><li>Pick one. Good enough beats perfect.</li></ul></div>
    <div><div class="t">Sun AM</div><div class="h">Choose what to buy (45 min)</div>
      <ul><li>Read chapters 9 to 11 <span class="pg">p.11&ndash;13</span></li><li>Shortlist one or two broad, low-cost index funds offered by your provider</li><li>Choose your monthly amount</li></ul></div>
    <div><div class="t">Sun PM</div><div class="h">Open, fund, automate (60 min)</div>
      <ul><li>Open the account <span class="pg">p.14</span></li><li>Link your bank, transfer money, <strong>buy</strong> the fund</li><li>Set up the automatic monthly investment</li><li>Tick off the checklist <span class="pg">p.15</span></li></ul></div>
  </div>
  <h3>What to have ready</h3>
  <div class="grid2">
    <ul class="check">
      <li>Your Social Security number (or ITIN)</li>
      <li>A government photo ID (driver's license or passport)</li>
      <li>Your bank's routing and account numbers</li>
    </ul>
    <ul class="check">
      <li>Your employer's name and address</li>
      <li>Name, date of birth and contact details for a beneficiary</li>
      <li>Your workplace benefits login, if you have a plan at work</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="box tip"><strong>If you get stuck:</strong> it's completely fine to spread this over two weekends. The only real mistake is never starting, or opening the account and leaving the money in cash for a year (page 14 shows how to avoid that).</div>
  <div class="folio"><span>Open Your First Investing Account</span><span>3</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4. BEFORE YOU INVEST ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="kicker">Chapter 2</div>
  <h2>Before you invest: a sensible order</h2>
  <p class="lede">Investing is for money you won't need for at least five years. Before that, it helps to have the ground under you solid. Many people work through something like this:</p>
  <div class="step"><div class="n">1</div><div class="b"><h4 style="margin-top:4px">A starter cash buffer</h4><p>Some emergency savings in an insured bank or credit union account, so a surprise bill doesn't force you to sell investments at a bad moment or go into card debt. A common starting target is one month of essential bills, growing toward three to six months over time.</p></div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="n">2</div><div class="b"><h4 style="margin-top:4px">Capture any employer match</h4><p>If your workplace plan matches contributions, that match is part of your pay. Contributing enough to get all of it is one of the few no-brainers in personal finance, even while you work on other goals. Page 6 shows how matches work.</p></div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="n">3</div><div class="b"><h4 style="margin-top:4px">High-interest debt</h4><p>Paying off a credit card charging around 20% to 30% is a guaranteed saving of that rate. No investment reliably beats that, so many people clear high-rate debt before investing beyond the match. Low-rate debt (say under 5%) is more of a judgment call.</p></div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="n">4</div><div class="b"><h4 style="margin-top:4px">Then: invest regularly</h4><p>An IRA, more in your workplace plan, or a taxable account, depending on the next few chapters. The habit (a fixed amount, every payday, automatically) matters more than the starting amount.</p></div></div>
  <h3>Quick self-check</h3>
  <table class="tight">
    <tr><th>Question</th><th style="width:1.2in">Yes / No</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Do I have some emergency cash that isn't invested?</td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Am I getting my full employer match (or is there none)?</td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Are my credit cards paid in full each month (or on a payoff plan)?</td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Is this money I won't need for at least five years?</td><td></td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="small">Four yeses: you're in a good spot to start this weekend. A no doesn't mean you can't invest; it just tells you what else deserves attention. This order is a common rule of thumb, not a rule. Your situation may call for a different one.</p>
  <div class="folio"><span>Open Your First Investing Account</span><span>4</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5. ACCOUNT TYPES ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="kicker">Chapter 3</div>
  <h2>The account types at a glance</h2>
  <p class="lede">An account is the container; the investments (like index funds) go inside it. The container decides how the money is taxed and when you can take it out.</p>
  <table class="acct">
    <tr><th></th><th>401(k) / 403(b)</th><th>Traditional IRA</th><th>Roth IRA</th><th>Taxable brokerage</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Who opens it</td><td>Your employer offers it; you enroll</td><td>You, at a provider you choose</td><td>You, at a provider you choose</td><td>You, at a provider you choose</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Who can contribute</td><td>Eligible employees, via payroll</td><td>Anyone with earned income (or a spouse who has it)</td><td>Earned income, below an income limit <span class="verify">verify</span></td><td>Anyone. No income test, no cap</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tax when money goes in</td><td>Traditional: pre-tax, lowers taxable pay now. Many plans also offer a Roth option (after-tax).</td><td>May be tax-deductible, depending on income and workplace plan coverage <span class="verify">verify</span></td><td>No deduction: after-tax money</td><td>No deduction: after-tax money</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tax while it grows</td><td>None</td><td>None</td><td>None</td><td>Dividends and realized gains taxed each year</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tax when you take it out</td><td>Traditional: taxed as income. Roth: qualified withdrawals tax-free</td><td>Taxed as ordinary income</td><td>Qualified withdrawals tax-free; your contributions can come out any time</td><td>Only on gains; long-term gains usually taxed at lower rates</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Early withdrawal</td><td>Before 59&frac12;, generally income tax + 10% additional tax, with exceptions</td><td>Same general rule, with exceptions</td><td>Contributions: anytime. Earnings: rules apply</td><td>Anytime (tax on any gain)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Employer match</td><td><strong>Often, yes</strong></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Investment menu</td><td>Limited to the plan's list</td><td>Wide: whatever the provider offers</td><td>Wide: whatever the provider offers</td><td>Wide: whatever the provider offers</td></tr>
  </table>
  <div class="box tip"><strong>The one-line version.</strong> Workplace plan: match + payroll automation. IRA: tax benefits + your choice of provider and funds. Taxable: flexibility, no limits, no tax perks. Many people end up with more than one; you don't need them all this weekend.</div>
  <p class="small">Simplified for clarity. Rules on deductions, income limits, early-withdrawal exceptions and required withdrawals have details this table can't show. Check irs.gov (search "IRA FAQs" and "401(k) plans") for the current rules.</p>
  <div class="folio"><span>Open Your First Investing Account</span><span>5</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6. WORKPLACE PLANS ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="kicker">Chapter 4</div>
  <h2>Workplace plans: 401(k) and 403(b)</h2>
  <p class="lede">A 401(k) (companies) or 403(b) (schools, hospitals, non-profits) lets you invest straight from your paycheck. If you have one, it's usually the first place to look.</p>
  <h3>How the match works</h3>
  <p>Many employers add money when you contribute, up to a limit. Two common formulas, for someone earning {{ salary|money }} a year (illustrative):</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Match formula</th><th class="r">You put in (per year)</th><th class="r">Employer adds</th><th class="r">Total invested</th></tr>
    {% for x in match %}<tr><td>{{ x.label }}</td><td class="r">{{ x.you|money }}</td><td class="r"><strong>{{ x.emp|money }}</strong></td><td class="r">{{ (x.you + x.emp)|money }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </table>
  <p>Contribute less than the match limit and that extra pay simply isn't paid. Your plan's exact formula is in the plan documents or benefits portal; look for "employer match" or "matching contribution".</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div>
      <h4>Vesting</h4>
      <p>Your own contributions are always yours. The employer's match may "vest" over a few years: leave before then and you could forfeit some of it. The plan's summary plan description tells you the schedule.</p>
      <h4>Traditional or Roth 401(k)?</h4>
      <p>If your plan offers both, the same question as for IRAs applies: pay tax now (Roth) or later (Traditional). See page 7. Employer match money has traditionally gone in pre-tax; check how your plan handles it.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4>What to pick inside the plan</h4>
      <p>Plans offer a menu. Look for low-cost broad index funds or a target-date fund near the year you expect to retire. Check each fund's <strong>expense ratio</strong> (page 12). If you do nothing, money may go into a default fund; check what it is.</p>
      <h4>Changing jobs</h4>
      <p>Your vested balance stays yours. You can usually leave it, move it to a new employer's plan, or roll it into an IRA. Avoid cashing out: taxes and penalties can take a big bite.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="box dark"><div class="kicker">This weekend</div><p style="margin:0">Log in to your benefits portal. Note: is there a plan, is there a match, what's the formula, what are you contributing now? If you're below the match, raising your contribution percentage takes five minutes and may be the most valuable thing you do all weekend.</p></div>
  <div class="folio"><span>Open Your First Investing Account</span><span>6</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 7. IRAs ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="kicker">Chapter 5</div>
  <h2>IRAs: Roth or Traditional?</h2>
  <p class="lede">An IRA (individual retirement account) is one you open yourself, at the provider of your choice. Both types grow without yearly tax. The difference is <em>when</em> you pay tax.</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card"><div class="lbl">Traditional IRA</div><p style="margin:4px 0 0">Contributions may be deductible now (lowering this year's tax). Withdrawals in retirement are taxed as income. <strong>Tax later.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="lbl">Roth IRA</div><p style="margin:4px 0 0">No deduction now. Qualified withdrawals in retirement are tax-free, including all the growth. <strong>Tax now.</strong></p></div>
  </div>
  <h3>The one question: is your tax rate higher now, or later?</h3>
  <p>Take {{ pre|money }} of pre-tax income and assume it grows {{ "%.1f"|format(growth30) }}&times; over 30 years (a hypothetical {{ "%.0f"|format(rate) }}% a year). Today's tax rate is 22%. What you'd have after tax:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Your tax rate in retirement</th><th class="r">Traditional: invest {{ pre|money }}, pay tax at the end</th><th class="r">Roth: pay 22% now, invest {{ (pre*0.78)|money }}</th><th>Better here</th></tr>
    {% for x in rt %}<tr><td>{{ "%.0f"|format(x.later) }}% ({{ ["lower than now","same as now","higher than now"][loop.index0] }})</td><td class="r">{{ x.trad|money }}</td><td class="r">{{ x.roth|money }}</td><td>{% if x.trad > x.roth + 1 %}Traditional{% elif x.roth > x.trad + 1 %}Roth{% else %}Tie{% endif %}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </table>
  <p>When the rates are equal, the result is identical. So the choice is really a guess about your future tax rate. Early in a career, when income (and tax rate) is often lower than it will be later, many people lean Roth. In high-earning years, the Traditional deduction is worth more. Plenty of people split the difference and use both over time.</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div>
      <h4>Other Roth features</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Your <strong>contributions</strong> (not earnings) can be withdrawn any time without tax or penalty.</li>
        <li>Earnings are tax-free once you're 59&frac12; and the account has been open five years (with some exceptions).</li>
        <li>No required withdrawals during the original owner's lifetime.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4>Rules to check <span class="verify">verify</span></h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Roth IRA has an income limit; above it you can't contribute directly.</li>
        <li>Traditional IRA deductions phase out at certain incomes if you (or a spouse) have a workplace plan.</li>
        <li>You need earned income at least equal to what you contribute (spousal IRAs are the exception).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="small">Illustration only: one lump sum, a fixed made-up return and flat tax rates. Real outcomes depend on your income, deductions, state taxes and future tax law. Not tax advice.</p>
  <div class="folio"><span>Open Your First Investing Account</span><span>7</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 8. TAXABLE + LIMITS ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="kicker">Chapter 6</div>
  <h2>Taxable brokerage accounts</h2>
  <p class="lede">A regular (taxable, "individual") brokerage account has no tax perks and no rules about who, how much, or when. That flexibility is the point.</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div>
      <h4>When it can make sense</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>You've used the tax-advantaged room you want to use this year.</li>
        <li>The goal is 5+ years away but isn't retirement (a home deposit in 7 years, say).</li>
        <li>You want money you can reach at any age without retirement-account rules.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4>How it's taxed (simplified)</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Dividends</strong> are taxed in the year you receive them, even if reinvested.</li>
        <li>When you sell for a profit, the gain is taxed. Held over one year: <strong>long-term</strong>, usually lower rates. One year or less: <strong>short-term</strong>, taxed like income.</li>
        <li>Your provider sends a tax form (1099) each year.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>Broad index funds tend to be relatively tax-efficient because they trade less, which is one reason they're popular in taxable accounts.</p>

  <h3>2026 contribution limits <span class="verify">verify at irs.gov</span></h3>
  <p>The IRS updates these every year, usually in the fall. These are the 2026 figures as announced; check irs.gov (search "401(k) and IRA contribution limits 2026") before you rely on them.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Account</th><th class="r">2026 limit</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    <tr><td>401(k), 403(b), most 457 plans, TSP: your contributions</td><td class="r"><strong>$24,500</strong></td><td>Employer match doesn't count toward this; a higher combined limit applies</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Catch-up, age 50+ (workplace plans)</td><td class="r">+$8,000</td><td>Higher catch-up for ages 60&ndash;63 ($11,250); some high earners must make catch-ups as Roth</td></tr>
    <tr><td>IRA (Traditional + Roth combined)</td><td class="r"><strong>$7,500</strong></td><td>Total across all your IRAs, not per account; can't exceed your earned income</td></tr>
    <tr><td>IRA catch-up, age 50+</td><td class="r">+$1,100</td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Roth IRA income phase-out</td><td class="r" style="white-space:nowrap">$153k&ndash;$168k<br>$242k&ndash;$252k</td><td>Modified AGI: single / head of household (first line), married filing jointly (second line)</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="small">IRA contributions for a tax year can usually be made until that year's tax-filing deadline (typically mid-April of the following year). Figures are for the 2026 tax year and may have been revised since this edition. Always confirm at irs.gov.</p>
  <div class="folio"><span>Open Your First Investing Account</span><span>8</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 9. COMPARE PROVIDERS ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="kicker">Chapter 7</div>
  <h2>How to compare providers</h2>
  <p class="lede">Any large, regulated brokerage can hold an IRA or a taxable account. You're not looking for "the best"; you're checking that nothing about the one you pick will quietly cost you. Ask each provider the same questions.</p>
  <table class="tight">
    <tr><th style="width:1.45in">What to check</th><th>Why it matters</th><th style="width:2.15in">What good looks like</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Account fees</strong></td><td>Annual, maintenance, inactivity or "low balance" fees eat small accounts alive.</td><td>None, at any balance</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Trading costs</strong></td><td>Commissions on buying ETFs or mutual funds add up with monthly investing.</td><td>No commission on the funds you'll buy</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Fund costs</strong></td><td>The expense ratio is charged every year, forever. It matters more than any one-off fee (page 12).</td><td>Broad index funds with very low expense ratios available</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Minimums</strong></td><td>Minimums to open the account, or to buy a particular fund, can block a small start.</td><td>$0 to open; fund minimums you can meet</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Fund choice</strong></td><td>You need at least one broad, diversified, low-cost option.</td><td>Total-market, S&amp;P 500, international, bond and target-date index funds or ETFs</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Automatic investing</strong></td><td>Automation is what makes the habit stick.</td><td>Recurring transfers <em>and</em> automatic purchases of your fund</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Fractional shares</strong></td><td>Lets every dollar get invested, even when a share costs more than you're adding.</td><td>Available for the funds you want</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Protection</strong></td><td>SIPC covers customers if a member brokerage firm fails (not investment losses).</td><td>SIPC member; check at sipc.org</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Leaving fees</strong></td><td>Some firms charge to transfer an account out.</td><td>Low or none; know the number</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Security &amp; service</strong></td><td>You'll log in for decades.</td><td>Two-factor login; reachable humans; an app you'll actually use</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Background</strong></td><td>Disciplinary history is public.</td><td>Clean record on FINRA BrokerCheck</td></tr>
  </table>
  <div class="box warn"><strong>Red flags:</strong> pressure to decide today; "guaranteed" returns; anyone recommending products they earn a commission on without telling you; a push toward complex products (options, leverage, crypto, high-fee annuities) for your first account; sign-up bonuses that require trading frequently.</div>
  <p class="small">Robo-advisors (automated portfolio services) and target-date funds both offer hands-off investing. Robo-advisors often add an advisory fee on top of fund costs; compare the all-in yearly cost.</p>
  <div class="folio"><span>Open Your First Investing Account</span><span>9</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 10. WORKSHEET ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="kicker">Printable</div>
  <h2>Provider comparison worksheet</h2>
  <p class="lede">Fill in one column per provider from their pricing and FAQ pages. If you can't find an answer in ten minutes, that's an answer too.</p>
  <table class="ws">
    <tr><th></th><th>Provider A</th><th>Provider B</th><th>Provider C</th></tr>
    {% for row in ["Name","Account fee (yearly / inactivity)","Minimum to open","Commission on ETFs / mutual funds","Lowest expense ratio: total-market or S&P 500 index fund","Target-date index funds? Expense ratio?","Fund minimum to buy","Fractional shares?","Automatic recurring investing?","Fee to transfer out","SIPC member? (sipc.org)","BrokerCheck: anything notable?","Two-factor login?","App / site: easy to use?","Account types offered (Roth, Traditional, taxable)","Gut feeling (1-5)"] %}
    <tr><td>{{ row }}</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </table>
  <div class="box tip"><strong>Decision rule:</strong> cross out any provider with account fees or commissions on the funds you'd buy. From the rest, pick the one with the lowest-cost broad index fund and automatic investing. If two tie, pick the app you like more. You'll use it for years.</div>
  <div class="folio"><span>Open Your First Investing Account</span><span>10</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 11. INDEX FUNDS ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="kicker">Chapter 9</div>
  <h2>Index fund basics</h2>
  <p class="lede">An index fund buys every investment in a list (an "index") in proportion, instead of paying someone to pick winners. One purchase gets you a slice of hundreds or thousands of companies.</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div>
      <h4>Why beginners like them</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Diversified:</strong> one company's bad year barely registers.</li>
        <li><strong>Low cost:</strong> no stock-picking team to pay, so expense ratios are often a fraction of actively managed funds.</li>
        <li><strong>Simple:</strong> you don't need opinions about individual companies.</li>
        <li><strong>Transparent:</strong> you know what you own.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4>What they don't do</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>They don't avoid market drops. When the market falls, a stock index fund falls with it, sometimes a lot, sometimes for years.</li>
        <li>They don't guarantee any return.</li>
        <li>A single-country stock fund is still concentrated in one market.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3>Common types</h3>
  <table class="tight">
    <tr><th style="width:1.7in">Type</th><th>What it holds</th><th style="width:1.9in">Typical role</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Total US stock market</strong></td><td>Practically every publicly traded US company, large to small</td><td>Core holding</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>S&amp;P 500</strong></td><td>About 500 large US companies</td><td>Core holding (large companies only)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Total international</strong></td><td>Companies outside the US, developed and emerging markets</td><td>Adds global diversification</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Total bond market</strong></td><td>Many government and corporate bonds</td><td>Steadier; smaller drops and lower expected growth</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Target-date fund</strong></td><td>A mix of the above that shifts from stocks toward bonds as the target year approaches</td><td>All-in-one, hands-off</td></tr>
  </table>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div>
      <h4>ETF or mutual fund?</h4>
      <p>Both can track the same index. <strong>ETFs</strong> trade during the day like a stock, usually with no fund minimum. <strong>Index mutual funds</strong> trade once a day at the closing price, sometimes with a minimum first purchase, and are easy to automate in fixed dollar amounts. For a long-term investor, either works. Compare expense ratios.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4>How many funds do I need?</h4>
      <p>Fewer than you'd think. Some people hold one target-date fund. Others hold a US fund and an international fund, adding a bond fund as they get closer to needing the money. More funds isn't more diversified if they all hold the same companies.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="small">Fund categories are described generally. Before buying any fund, read its summary prospectus or fact sheet: what it holds, its expense ratio and its risks.</p>
  <div class="folio"><span>Open Your First Investing Account</span><span>11</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 12. FEES ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="kicker">Chapter 10</div>
  <h2>Why fees matter, with numbers</h2>
  <p class="lede">The <strong>expense ratio</strong> is the yearly cost of a fund, as a percentage of what you have in it. It's taken out quietly, so you never see a bill. That's exactly why it's worth checking.</p>
  <div class="formula">yearly cost &asymp; amount invested &times; expense ratio<br>e.g. $10,000 &times; 0.50% = $50 a year &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; $10,000 &times; 0.05% = $5 a year</div>
  <p>$45 a year sounds like nothing. But the fee is charged on your whole balance, every year, and the money it takes can't compound for you. Here's {{ 300|money }} a month for 30 years, assuming a hypothetical {{ "%.0f"|format(rate) }}% a year before fees:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Expense ratio</th><th class="r">Yearly cost per $10,000</th><th class="r">Balance after 30 years</th><th class="r">Less than the 0.05% fund</th></tr>
    {% for f in fees %}<tr{% if loop.first %} class="sub"{% endif %}><td>{{ "%.2f"|format(f.fee) }}%</td><td class="r">{{ f.yearly_on_10k|money }}</td><td class="r"><strong>{{ f.fv|money }}</strong></td><td class="r">{% if loop.first %}&ndash;{% else %}{{ f.lost|money }}{% endif %}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </table>
  <p>Same contributions ({{ (300*360)|money }} in total), same market. The only difference is the fee: about {{ fees[2].lost|money }} less with a 1% fee than with a 0.05% one.</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div>
      <h4>Where to find it</h4>
      <p>On the fund's page at your provider, its fact sheet, or its prospectus. Look for "expense ratio" or "net expense ratio".</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4>Other costs to look out for</h4>
      <p>Sales charges ("loads"), account fees, advisory fees (common with managed accounts and some robo-advisors) and trading commissions. Add them up to compare the all-in yearly cost.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="small">Calculation: monthly contributions at the end of each month, monthly compounding, fee approximated by subtracting it from the assumed annual return. The {{ "%.0f"|format(rate) }}% return is a made-up round number for illustration, not a prediction. Real returns vary year to year and can be negative.</p>
  <div class="folio"><span>Open Your First Investing Account</span><span>12</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 13. COMPOUNDING ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="kicker">Chapter 11</div>
  <h2>Compounding, worked examples</h2>
  <p class="lede">Compounding means your returns start earning returns. It's slow at first and then surprisingly fast, which is why time in the market matters more than the starting amount.</p>
  <div class="chart">
    <div style="font-weight:700;font-size:10pt">{{ 250|money }} a month for 40 years at a hypothetical {{ "%.0f"|format(rate) }}% a year</div>
    <svg viewBox="-56 -10 676 300" style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block" aria-label="Growth chart">
      {% for y, v in chart.grid %}<line x1="0" x2="600" y1="{{ y }}" y2="{{ y }}" stroke="#e5e0d2" stroke-width="1"/><text x="-8" y="{{ y + 4 }}" text-anchor="end" font-size="11" fill="#4b5a51" font-family="Inter">{{ (v/1000)|int }}k</text>{% endfor %}
      <polygon points="{{ chart.total }}" fill="#2fbf71"/>
      <polygon points="{{ chart.contrib }}" fill="#f2b134"/>
      <line x1="0" y1="260" x2="600" y2="260" stroke="#0b1d13" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      {% for yr in [0,10,20,30,40] %}<text x="{{ yr*15 }}" y="278" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#4b5a51" font-family="Inter">{{ yr }} yrs</text>{% endfor %}
      <text x="596" y="22" text-anchor="end" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#0b1d13" font-family="Inter">{{ chart.end|money }}</text>
    </svg>
    <div class="legend"><span><i style="background:#f2b134"></i>What you put in ({{ chart.end_in|money }})</span><span><i style="background:#2fbf71"></i>Growth ({{ (chart.end - chart.end_in)|money }})</span><span>At 20 years: {{ chart.mid[1]|money }} from {{ chart.mid[2]|money }} put in</span></div>
  </div>
  <h3>Monthly amounts over time (hypothetical {{ "%.0f"|format(rate) }}% a year)</h3>
  <table class="tight">
    <tr><th>Per month</th>{% for y in years %}<th class="r">{{ y }} years</th>{% endfor %}</tr>
    {% for g in grid %}<tr><td><strong>{{ g.pmt|money }}</strong></td>{% for c in g.cells %}<td class="r">{{ c.fv|money }}<br><span class="small">put in {{ c["in"]|money }}</span></td>{% endfor %}</tr>{% endfor %}
  </table>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div>
      <h4>The return assumption changes a lot</h4>
      <table class="tight">
        <tr><th>$200/mo, 30 yrs</th><th class="r">Ends at</th></tr>
        {% for r in rate_rows %}<tr><td>{{ "%.0f"|format(r.rate*100) }}% a year</td><td class="r">{{ r.fv|money }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
        <tr><td>Total put in</td><td class="r">{{ rate_rows[0]["in"]|money }}</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4>Starting early (made-up people)</h4>
      <p style="font-size:9.2pt">Alex invests $200/month from 25 to 35, then stops: {{ early.a_in|money }} in. Sam invests $200/month from 35 to 65: {{ early.b_in|money }} in. At 65, at {{ "%.0f"|format(rate) }}%: Alex has about <strong>{{ early.a65|money }}</strong>, Sam about <strong>{{ early.b65|money }}</strong>. Alex put in a third as much and ends up close.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="small">All figures computed with monthly contributions at the end of each month and monthly compounding, before taxes, fees and inflation. Returns are assumptions, not predictions; real returns vary every year and can be negative for long stretches. Try your own numbers at investor.gov (compound interest calculator).</p>
  <div class="folio"><span>Open Your First Investing Account</span><span>13</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 14. STEP BY STEP ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="kicker">Chapter 12</div>
  <h2>Opening the account, step by step</h2>
  <p class="lede">The exact screens vary by provider, but every one follows roughly this path. Allow about 30 minutes, plus a few business days for money to arrive.</p>
  <div class="step"><div class="n">1</div><div class="b"><h4 style="margin-top:4px">Choose the account type</h4><p>"Roth IRA", "Traditional IRA" or "Individual brokerage". Opening an account doesn't commit you to investing any amount yet.</p></div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="n">2</div><div class="b"><h4 style="margin-top:4px">Enter your details</h4><p>Name, address, date of birth, SSN, employment and some financial questions. These are legally required (to verify identity and assess suitability). Answer honestly.</p></div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="n">3</div><div class="b"><h4 style="margin-top:4px">Name a beneficiary</h4><p>Who inherits the account. It's quick and easy to skip, and skipping it can create a mess for your family. Add a primary and, if you can, a contingent beneficiary.</p></div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="n">4</div><div class="b"><h4 style="margin-top:4px">Turn on security</h4><p>Two-factor authentication and a unique password. Never share login codes with anyone who contacts you.</p></div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="n">5</div><div class="b"><h4 style="margin-top:4px">Link your bank and transfer money</h4><p>With routing and account numbers or a bank login. For an IRA, choose which <strong>tax year</strong> the contribution is for if asked.</p></div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="n">6</div><div class="b"><h4 style="margin-top:4px">Buy your investment. Don't skip this.</h4><p>Money you transfer usually lands in a cash "settlement" or "core" position. <strong>It isn't invested until you buy something.</strong> Search for your chosen fund, enter a dollar amount and confirm. A surprising number of people find their IRA sat in cash for years.</p></div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="n">7</div><div class="b"><h4 style="margin-top:4px">Automate it</h4><p>Set a recurring transfer from your bank on payday <em>and</em> an automatic investment into your fund. Check both are switched on; some providers treat them separately.</p></div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="n">8</div><div class="b"><h4 style="margin-top:4px">Then leave it alone</h4><p>Check in a few times a year, raise the amount when your pay goes up, and resist selling when the market drops. Drops are normal and expected.</p></div></div>
  <div class="folio"><span>Open Your First Investing Account</span><span>14</span></div>
</section>

