<!-- ============ 16. GLOSSARY ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="kicker">Chapter 14</div>
  <h2>Glossary</h2>
  <dl class="gl">
    <dt>401(k) / 403(b)</dt><dd>Employer-sponsored retirement plans funded from your paycheck. 403(b)s are for schools, hospitals and non-profits.</dd>
    <dt>Asset allocation</dt><dd>How your money is split between stocks, bonds and cash. The biggest driver of both growth and how bumpy the ride is.</dd>
    <dt>Beneficiary</dt><dd>The person (or people) who inherit an account. Named on the account, separate from a will.</dd>
    <dt>Bond</dt><dd>A loan to a government or company that pays interest. Usually steadier than stocks, with lower expected returns.</dd>
    <dt>Brokerage account</dt><dd>An account for buying and holding investments. "Taxable" or "individual" means no special tax treatment.</dd>
    <dt>Capital gain</dt><dd>Profit when you sell for more than you paid. Long-term (held over a year) is usually taxed at lower rates than short-term.</dd>
    <dt>Compounding</dt><dd>Earning returns on previous returns, so growth builds on itself over time.</dd>
    <dt>Diversification</dt><dd>Spreading money across many investments so no single one can sink you.</dd>
    <dt>Dividend</dt><dd>A share of company profits paid to shareholders. Funds pass them on; you can reinvest them automatically.</dd>
    <dt>Dollar-cost averaging</dt><dd>Investing a fixed amount on a schedule, regardless of price. It's what automatic monthly investing does.</dd>
    <dt>Earned income</dt><dd>Pay from work (wages, self-employment). Needed to contribute to an IRA.</dd>
    <dt>ETF</dt><dd>Exchange-traded fund: a fund that trades during the day like a stock.</dd>
    <dt>Expense ratio</dt><dd>A fund's yearly cost, as a percentage of your balance in it.</dd>
    <dt>Fiduciary</dt><dd>Someone legally required to act in your best interest. Ask any adviser whether they are one, at all times, in writing.</dd>
    <dt>Fractional shares</dt><dd>Buying part of a share, so every dollar can be invested.</dd>
    <dt>Index</dt><dd>A list of investments that represents a market (for example, the S&amp;P 500).</dd>
    <dt>Index fund</dt><dd>A fund that holds everything in an index, rather than picking investments.</dd>
    <dt>IRA</dt><dd>Individual retirement account, opened by you. Traditional (tax later) or Roth (tax now).</dd>
    <dt>Match</dt><dd>Money your employer adds to your workplace plan when you contribute.</dd>
    <dt>Mutual fund</dt><dd>A pooled fund priced once a day. Can be index or actively managed.</dd>
    <dt>Rebalancing</dt><dd>Selling a bit of what's grown and buying what's lagged to get back to your chosen mix.</dd>
    <dt>RMD</dt><dd>Required minimum distribution: yearly withdrawals the IRS requires from most pre-tax retirement accounts from a certain age. Roth IRAs have none for the original owner.</dd>
    <dt>Roth 401(k)</dt><dd>A Roth option inside a workplace plan: after-tax contributions, tax-free qualified withdrawals.</dd>
    <dt>Rollover</dt><dd>Moving a retirement account to another provider or plan without triggering tax, when done correctly.</dd>
    <dt>Settlement fund</dt><dd>The cash holding where deposits land before you buy. Not the same as being invested.</dd>
    <dt>SIPC</dt><dd>Securities Investor Protection Corporation. Protects customers of a member firm that fails, within limits. Doesn't protect against market losses.</dd>
    <dt>Target-date fund</dt><dd>An all-in-one fund that gets more conservative as its target year approaches.</dd>
    <dt>Vesting</dt><dd>When employer contributions become fully yours. Your own contributions are always 100% vested.</dd>
  </dl>
  <div class="folio"><span>Open Your First Investing Account</span><span>16</span></div>
</section>

