  <h2>Where to verify, and the fine print</h2>
  <p class="lede">Rules and limits change. These official and non-commercial sources are where to check anything in this guide before you act on it.</p>
  <table class="tight">
    <tr><th style="width:1.8in">Source</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>irs.gov</strong></td><td>Current contribution limits, Roth IRA income limits, IRA deduction rules, early-withdrawal exceptions, required distributions. Search "IRA FAQs", "Retirement topics" and "401(k) and IRA contribution limits".</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>investor.gov</strong> (SEC)</td><td>Plain-English guides to funds, fees and fraud, plus a free compound interest calculator.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>brokercheck.finra.org</strong></td><td>Look up a brokerage firm or financial professional's registration and disciplinary history.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>sipc.org</strong></td><td>Check whether a brokerage is a SIPC member and what SIPC protection does and doesn't cover.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>dol.gov</strong> (EBSA)</td><td>Your rights in employer retirement plans, including fee disclosures and vesting.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Your plan documents</strong></td><td>Your employer's match formula, vesting schedule, fund menu and fees: the summary plan description and annual fee disclosure.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <h3>How the examples were calculated</h3>
  <p>Every number in this guide's examples was computed, not estimated: monthly contributions added at the end of each month, monthly compounding at the stated annual rate divided by 12, no taxes, no inflation. Fee examples subtract the expense ratio from the assumed annual return. The people (Alex, Sam) and the {{ salary|money }} salary are made up. Assumed returns of 4%, 6% and 8% a year are round numbers chosen to show sensitivity; they aren't forecasts and they aren't based on any particular fund.</p>
